{"id": 105, "ts": 1780436951.0902023, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I hear Sarah and James leaning toward Candidate\u202fC, largely because of the quick, correct decisions and strong attention to detail you\u2019ve noted. Those are certainly valuable for decision quality, but I\u2019m uneasy about the reported communication difficulties and egocentric attitude. In a long\u2011distance cockpit, the ability to convey ideas clearly and to collaborate without putting oneself above the crew is essential for crew cooperation and professional communication. Candidate\u202fC\u2019s reluctance to pursue further education also raises questions about adaptability and feedback orientation.\nBy contrast, Candidate\u202fB demonstrates solid stress resilience (\u201chandles stress very well\u201d) and a high level of conscientiousness, both key for operational reliability on long hauls. Their skill in assessing weather conditions directly supports safe international operations, and excellent computer skills suggest strong technical competence. While the \u201cpretentious\u201d perception and occasional tone issues are concerns, they appear less likely to undermine safety than the more fundamental teamwork deficits I see with Candidate\u202fC. Therefore, I maintain my provisional recommendation for Candidate\u202fB, believing the balance of reliability, stress resilience, and technical capability outweighs the communication style concerns."}
